  'This senior capstone integrates hospitality marketing, finance, accounting, operations, people, technology, real estate, law, and communication. Students diagnose industries and enterprises, evaluate resources and business models, choose competitive and corporate strategies, assess ownership and global-growth options, connect strategy to valuation and capital allocation, govern innovation and AI, design resilient and sustainable operating models, and lead implementation. A case-based strategy challenge culminates in a board-ready memo, analytical exhibits, presentation, and defense.',
  $hstmjson${"slug": "hospitality-strategic-management", "title": "Hospitality Strategic Management", "description": "This senior capstone integrates hospitality marketing, finance, accounting, operations, people, technology, real estate, law, and communication. Students diagnose industries and enterprises, evaluate resources and business models, choose competitive and corporate strategies, assess ownership and global-growth options, connect strategy to valuation and capital allocation, govern innovation and AI, design resilient and sustainable operating models, and lead implementation. A case-based strategy challenge culminates in a board-ready memo, analytical exhibits, presentation, and defense.", "topics": [{"id": "6505fb8a-c88c-4232-b41b-5c8c28f1e37d", "type": "topic", "title": "Strategic Management, Integrative Thinking, and Strategic Diagnosis", "description": "Strategic management asks how a hospitality enterprise can create durable value when guests, competitors, assets, talent, technology, capital, and institutions are changing. This opening section establishes a disciplined process for diagnosis, choice, execution, and learning.", "children": [{"id": "f5c7de7f-481d-4368-8770-b2bc707b1763", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The work of strategy", "children": [{"id": "4c1fe305-afa1-4d74-a714-ad35fbdb0586", "type": "concept", "title": "Mission, purpose, vision, values, objectives, strategy, policy, tactics, operating plan, and business model"}, {"id": "d29c2a50-8919-471f-914e-2e8763d287ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent action, choice, tradeoff, fit, positioning, advantage, and renewal"}, {"id": "c7b6bec9-5b53-4be2-887b-2f73e44beda4", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate, business-unit, brand, property, function, platform, destination, and ecosystem levels"}], "description": "Students distinguish strategy from goals, budgets, operational excellence, slogans, and isolated initiatives."}, {"id": "92c96383-1c8a-439b-a23d-1149cb6dba8e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Strategic problem framing", "children": [{"id": "2d1c413a-3186-4883-8632-50105754358f", "type": "concept", "title": "Symptoms versus causes, issue trees, hypotheses, assumptions, constraints, uncertainties, and decision rights"}, {"id": "f8d840ca-d8f1-464a-b66d-147619aaaae7", "type": "concept", "title": "Relevant time horizon, unit of analysis, stakeholder perspectives, boundaries, counterfactual, and opportunity cost"}, {"id": "6539d3e7-80ac-4315-bb45-7b0b07f585d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Facts, estimates, forecasts, scenarios, inference, judgment, bias, and confidence"}], "description": "This subtopic turns an ambiguous management situation into the key question, decision, evidence needs, alternatives, and criteria."}, {"id": "d6f91b9f-60cd-46d9-8dff-e5d8d5c46afc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Integrated hospitality diagnosis", "children": [{"id": "78459be8-449f-4846-981f-f99a026da493", "type": "concept", "title": "Guest promise, service system, demand, capacity, channel, labor, asset, capital, owner, and community linkages"}, {"id": "596991e1-7e0c-486c-9d6f-c51c4df8a039", "type": "concept", "title": "Key performance drivers, reinforcing loops, bottlenecks, tensions, second-order effects, and unintended consequences"}, {"id": "beab762e-dd36-4a53-bbd4-e3692e0cbb47", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic narrative, evidence table, material issues, priorities, and concise executive problem statement"}], "description": "Students connect marketing, operations, people, technology, finance, real estate, law, and sustainability into one enterprise view."}]}, {"id": "5c9ea195-3358-48b1-955a-02bb4c7987aa", "type": "topic", "title": "External Environment, Industry Structure, and Hospitality Ecosystems", "description": "No firm chooses strategy in a vacuum. Students assess macro forces, industry economics, competitors, complementors, platforms, regulators, and local stakeholders to identify structural opportunities and threats.", "children": [{"id": "c990ae6f-5378-4f6d-bcbe-2a537fc5ee98", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Macroenvironment and scenarios", "children": [{"id": "f8f56e67-903f-4682-833f-894de285733d", "type": "concept", "title": "Political, economic, social, technological, environmental, and legal analysis"}, {"id": "ca3b7937-fe1b-4bef-b0b4-2f77047e2fa7", "type": "concept", "title": "Demographics, mobility, work patterns, inflation, labor, interest rates, health, climate, geopolitics, and regulation"}, {"id": "5efc4354-11ac-428b-bdd5-0ca8d3530efc", "type": "concept", "title": "Predetermined elements, critical uncertainties, weak signals, scenario axes, narratives, implications, and signposts"}], "description": "Students examine forces that can reshape demand, cost, access, investment, and legitimacy."}, {"id": "d64518f2-a873-4fcf-9d5b-75b2fc93e784", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Industry structure and profit pools", "children": [{"id": "4188a654-05fb-40f9-8937-75af91e85693", "type": "concept", "title": "Five forces, market definition, strategic groups, concentration, entry barriers, switching costs, and exit barriers"}, {"id": "3ecaa984-f76d-499f-9ecc-730460b47c72", "type": "concept", "title": "Brand, owner, operator, franchisee, landlord, lender, OTA, GDS, supplier, employee, and guest bargaining power"}, {"id": "9dc5858a-200d-49ff-a244-23be9a2e6676", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenue pools, cost pools, margin pools, channel leakage, asset intensity, cycle, maturity, and structural change"}], "description": "This subtopic explains how bargaining power, rivalry, entry, substitutes, complements, and capacity shape value capture."}, {"id": "b9d89191-c4f4-4dab-8ebc-02990332564c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Competitors, platforms, and ecosystems", "children": [{"id": "19466fa4-e6fd-428c-b6d8-cedac480f06c", "type": "concept", "title": "Competitive set, strategic-group map, benchmarking, relative cost, differentiation, moves, countermoves, and game theory"}, {"id": "9caeed64-b88a-47e9-b2c8-0a5ebf99ff65", "type": "concept", "title": "Airlines, destinations, attractions, mobility, payment, loyalty, technology, media, food delivery, and experience partners"}, {"id": "db65d5ba-67b2-4825-b481-56e228539be0", "type": "concept", "title": "Network effects, standards, data access, multihoming, orchestration, complementors, coopetition, and ecosystem health"}], "description": "Students analyze rivalry and collaboration across interconnected hospitality and travel systems."}]}, {"id": "b4bacfc9-3e5a-457d-a8e0-3d84f7a625b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Resources, Capabilities, Value Chains, and Organizational Advantage", "description": "External opportunity matters only when the organization can act. Students identify the tangible, intangible, human, relational, and organizational resources that produce distinctive guest and financial outcomes.", "children": [{"id": "77f1f73f-670a-47b0-ab90-d93981b34370", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Resource and capability analysis", "children": [{"id": "ee8d759c-f9d3-416b-88d8-12233080adc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Physical assets, locations, capital, data, technology, brands, contracts, intellectual property, culture, talent, and relationships"}, {"id": "c1a06fbf-da54-426e-82b9-3298d846e530", "type": "concept", "title": "Resource versus capability, routines, systems, dynamic capabilities, absorptive capacity, learning, and path dependence"}, {"id": "99492fba-a38b-4898-9346-860c4046cb23", "type": "concept", "title": "Value, rarity, imitability, organization, durability, transferability, substitutability, and appropriation"}], "description": "Students evaluate what the enterprise owns, accesses, coordinates, learns, and protects."}, {"id": "7a6bdff8-08b7-4534-b5de-f630b64223f8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Value chain and activity-system analysis", "children": [{"id": "baf1096a-ed7c-4eaf-a846-701dac0c3536", "type": "concept", "title": "Development, procurement, distribution, marketing, booking, arrival, operations, service, recovery, loyalty, and support activities"}, {"id": "e5d5ef2f-14c2-46e1-8bf3-27cedb4f655d", "type": "concept", "title": "Outsource versus own, shared services, clusters, centralization, local adaptation, interfaces, handoffs, and bottlenecks"}, {"id": "0e2b32a7-9518-40d9-9908-58c76119eb15", "type": "concept", "title": "Activity fit, complementarities, tradeoffs, cost drivers, willingness-to-pay drivers, leakage, and redesign"}], "description": "This subtopic maps how activities combine to deliver the guest promise and where cost, quality, speed, risk, and differentiation arise."}, {"id": "29c08560-35ac-40b1-be00-65c99cf91296", "type": "subtopic", "title": "People, culture, and relational capability", "children": [{"id": "159ed741-16ad-4e38-9478-a6a052277569", "type": "concept", "title": "Leadership, psychological safety, employee value proposition, skills, staffing model, incentives, voice, and retention"}, {"id": "29a0a352-263c-49c3-a8ab-f95e01f4e4af", "type": "concept", "title": "Service climate, norms, stories, rituals, identity, inclusion, accountability, learning, and ethical culture"}, {"id": "b21fe87c-7c5f-4ce5-a427-41e1fe7df5c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Owner-operator, brand-franchisee, labor, vendor, community, government, and partner relationships"}], "description": "Students treat service culture and stakeholder relationships as strategic systems rather than soft background conditions."}]}, {"id": "1091e6a2-2f51-4761-8937-65c5420e7af5", "type": "topic", "title": "Business Models, Competitive Positioning, and Advantage", "description": "A business model explains who receives value, how it is delivered, and how the enterprise captures enough value to sustain the system. Students compare alternative positions and test whether choices reinforce one another.", "children": [{"id": "92826e62-89d4-4ddc-b92b-2d14322ac94f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hospitality business-model design", "children": [{"id": "4bd2a230-abac-44a1-9abc-352ff54e83f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Target guests, jobs-to-be-done, value proposition, channels, relationships, key activities, resources, and partners"}, {"id": "57fb6e89-ff7a-48a2-b293-c0cb6faa2442", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenue model, pricing, unit economics, cost structure, working capital, asset intensity, scalability, and risk allocation"}, {"id": "8752124d-60d8-4b2a-ad4b-9bee57736d3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Owned, leased, franchised, managed, licensed, membership, subscription, marketplace, aggregator, and hybrid models"}], "description": "Students specify the customer, proposition, activities, resources, partners, economics, and governance of a hospitality concept."}, {"id": "8adfe6e6-7c60-46fd-bbd6-4b849a37ee05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cost leadership, differentiation, and focus", "children": [{"id": "cd148401-4c43-4dab-8b62-bf1c885a1b74", "type": "concept", "title": "Relative cost, economies of scale/scope/density/learning, capacity utilization, process design, and procurement"}, {"id": "5a8316e2-58bf-4fb6-953f-0446105e973b", "type": "concept", "title": "Brand meaning, experience, design, convenience, personalization, trust, location, community, quality, and innovation"}, {"id": "8812acc6-8139-40b3-aa65-3e241a39b341", "type": "concept", "title": "Broad versus focused scope, best-cost positions, strategic ambiguity, imitation, erosion, and being stuck in the middle"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how firms create superior value through a deliberately configured activity system."}, {"id": "12adca9a-cf94-44dc-b077-9cb30d7fb81f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Value innovation and strategy testing", "children": [{"id": "17e4e41f-2862-4b28-8c6f-8d53f6824aed", "type": "concept", "title": "Value curves, eliminate-reduce-raise-create, noncustomers, substitutes, complements, and pain points"}, {"id": "72992ecf-6ead-4206-a875-450d5d266485", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, tradeoffs, consistency, reinforcing activities, isolating mechanisms, and response scenarios"}, {"id": "998cab54-2f11-47da-ba96-b18aa9cd94bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept tests, pilots, minimum viable business model, leading indicators, kill criteria, and learning agenda"}], "description": "Students challenge inherited industry assumptions and test whether a proposed position is coherent, desirable, feasible, viable, and defensible."}]}, {"id": "ee1943c9-3196-4da7-b214-95503370572c", "type": "topic", "title": "Growth, Diversification, Vertical Scope, M&A, and Alliances", "description": "Growth creates value only when the firm has an advantage in the new activity and the benefits exceed complexity, capital, and execution risk. Students evaluate organic and inorganic pathways with explicit parenting logic.", "children": [{"id": "dd9e6042-a591-4aed-a65b-0d63a35f4de2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Organic growth and portfolio pathways", "children": [{"id": "070e1974-2a3d-4f46-aed2-55f630841d42", "type": "concept", "title": "Market penetration, product development, market development, adjacency, replication, conversion, and new concept creation"}, {"id": "2db19938-1c58-4401-a425-6f1ddc167736", "type": "concept", "title": "Same-store growth, new units, white space, pipeline, ramp-up, cannibalization, saturation, and learning curve"}, {"id": "a466b9b9-f0c3-41dd-9736-dfca92ddda74", "type": "concept", "title": "Core, adjacent, transformational horizons; options, sequencing, stage gates, and resource commitments"}], "description": "Students compare deeper penetration, new segments, new markets, new offers, and adjacent services."}, {"id": "c8b647a1-6a16-4fe5-a542-4950a3070cb1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diversification and vertical integration", "children": [{"id": "49dbfabe-8d21-4984-8803-6314b724c934", "type": "concept", "title": "Related and unrelated diversification, economies of scope, shared activities, cross-selling, brand leverage, and internal capital markets"}, {"id": "1139de4d-0c79-43da-a390-44e96d6cbdff", "type": "concept", "title": "Backward and forward integration, transaction costs, hold-up, quality control, data, coordination, flexibility, and antitrust awareness"}, {"id": "c7194708-c56b-48bf-b209-934a90dea534", "type": "concept", "title": "Parenting advantage, better-off test, ownership test, complexity discount, bureaucracy, and divestiture"}], "description": "This subtopic tests when corporate ownership creates more value than market contracting."}, {"id": "d7a58011-8250-4fce-9348-f4acbe6344d5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mergers, acquisitions, joint ventures, and alliances", "children": [{"id": "b51ae0e5-f7a7-4e07-b948-dc05751bd4c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Build, buy, partner, license, franchise, joint venture, minority investment, management agreement, and strategic alliance"}, {"id": "db37cb37-99ca-40f2-9935-7d5dd0b5ba50", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, standalone value, synergies, control premium, diligence, financing, conditions, and downside case"}, {"id": "4bd42a8b-855a-48f4-a284-c991d73fd46f", "type": "concept", "title": "Integration thesis, culture, systems, talent, brand, customers, Day 1, milestones, synergy tracking, and exit"}], "description": "Students examine target logic, valuation discipline, deal structure, integration, and post-deal accountability."}]}, {"id": "73886c80-e5d0-4f42-87b8-c2eacd789cb6", "type": "topic", "title": "Brand Portfolio, Customer, Channel, and Commercial Strategy", "description": "Hospitality enterprises compete through portfolios of brands, relationships, channels, and revenue streams. Students align customer value and commercial systems with corporate strategy and property economics.", "children": [{"id": "cbf345e1-a317-4d03-a7a7-191dfb5e1fc0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Brand architecture and portfolio roles", "children": [{"id": "b6a0bf9f-35f8-4e4a-93c8-86cca2a470dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Branded house, house of brands, endorsed brands, soft brands, collections, flags, outlet brands, and co-brands"}, {"id": "1436db84-2478-4295-93a6-37c8341879aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Target, promise, price tier, occasion, geography, owner proposition, standards, and portfolio role"}, {"id": "675fe3db-74d0-4406-94d4-4a3fa7540f99", "type": "concept", "title": "Gap, overlap, cannibalization, extension, conversion, revitalization, retirement, and portfolio governance"}], "description": "Students decide how brands cover segments without creating costly overlap or confusing guests, owners, and employees."}, {"id": "bd686bb6-cc23-42ba-9b3c-9e5d25f3c954", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Customer and channel strategy", "children": [{"id": "975b4a66-0113-4aaa-9c0c-6a96cd29ffc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Segments, corporate accounts, groups, members, locals, residents, intermediaries, and lifetime value"}, {"id": "0f3cc29b-9e56-49de-be1a-3cec4e682d7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Direct, brand, OTA, GDS, wholesale, metasearch, delivery, membership, partnership, and loyalty channels"}, {"id": "664c9e63-7ea5-419e-bff5-8c38cd10adab", "type": "concept", "title": "Acquisition cost, commission, contribution, data access, retention, bargaining power, channel conflict, and incrementality"}], "description": "This subtopic evaluates which demand to attract, how to reach it, and what relationships are worth building."}, {"id": "0648d11c-4d7d-4dc3-8129-5896f501113d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Commercial operating model", "children": [{"id": "c6f18812-5d04-4e8b-b156-b9bdf0246d12", "type": "concept", "title": "Demand generation, pricing, inventory, group displacement, promotions, partnerships, and ancillary revenue"}, {"id": "35e55bcb-95ec-4a74-b284-aa2a3ed33c7b", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial organization, decision rights, shared metrics, incentives, meeting cadence, forecast, and accountability"}, {"id": "7ca3e050-f401-481d-8150-fea6b3411ba2", "type": "concept", "title": "Top-line growth versus profitable demand, brand equity, guest trust, operational readiness, and owner return"}], "description": "Students integrate marketing, sales, revenue management, distribution, CRM, and operations around shared enterprise goals."}]}, {"id": "f8b9621f-9153-4a96-b002-abdbfc7009a7", "type": "topic", "title": "Ownership, Asset-Light Strategy, Contracts, and Hotel Asset Management", "description": "Hospitality strategy often separates real-estate ownership, brand, operations, distribution, and capital. Students learn how contracts allocate economics, control, risk, and incentives across the owner-brand-operator system.", "children": [{"id": "9ac82949-8707-471b-a974-f2f525445147", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ownership and operating structures", "children": [{"id": "17c43807-6f00-4737-a6bf-00492d790270", "type": "concept", "title": "Hotel owner, operator, brand, franchisee, franchisor, landlord, tenant, lender, asset manager, and third-party manager roles"}, {"id": "a6534e7a-cb6a-4510-8b84-2e4ebfe60b60", "type": "concept", "title": "Control, capital intensity, fee streams, residual claims, guarantees, key money, territorial rights, and risk sharing"}, {"id": "5df5b04f-c423-4db9-917d-b01755f579de", "type": "concept", "title": "Management agreements, franchise agreements, leases, licenses, SLAs, covenants, and termination rights"}], "description": "Students compare owned, leased, managed, franchised, licensed, and affiliated arrangements."}, {"id": "ecbe0f43-42bc-4478-8d87-2731cd2915c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asset-light and capital-light strategy", "children": [{"id": "57647c33-f929-4c87-b5a3-7a7e7fe660f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Fee growth, return on invested capital, scalability, volatility, balance-sheet capacity, brand reach, and pipeline"}, {"id": "9fbc7434-239c-4fda-ad21-8f8d6dbfc41d", "type": "concept", "title": "Quality-control loss, owner dependence, contract rigidity, fee conflicts, capital support, reputational spillovers, and governance cost"}, {"id": "0b35e8d2-3a03-499f-a87e-6a439a1fc662", "type": "concept", "title": "Owned-versus-managed/franchised mix, master franchising, development partners, conversions, and platform economics"}], "description": "This subtopic explains why firms separate assets from operations and when that separation creates or destroys value."}, {"id": "5c1c5c3d-6b0e-45cd-886a-3bfe8e72f7de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asset-management and owner strategy", "children": [{"id": "55e022f0-e259-48c7-8f70-7e8a568a579a", "type": "concept", "title": "Market position, GOP, EBITDA-related measures, cash flow, reserve, debt, valuation, cap rate, and total return"}, {"id": "01644566-66be-432f-8e51-707b3a48bdb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Budget challenge, benchmarking, flow-through, labor, channel cost, capex, PIP, deferred maintenance, and sustainability investment"}, {"id": "e26a53b9-9a1b-4131-9792-8938479a448e", "type": "concept", "title": "Hold, sell, refinance, renovate, reposition, rebrand, convert, change operator, adaptive reuse, and exit timing"}], "description": "Students evaluate property performance, capital needs, brand/operator fit, and hold-sell-renovate-reposition decisions."}]}, {"id": "bb83908c-d224-4242-84b5-10d27880b127", "type": "topic", "title": "Global Expansion, Institutions, Culture, and Localization", "description": "International growth exposes firms to new demand and resources while increasing distance, institutional complexity, political risk, and cultural adaptation needs. Students learn to choose where, how, and at what pace to expand.", "children": [{"id": "2fe14201-0a3f-45f8-8400-434f4a1b1dd9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Country and city attractiveness", "children": [{"id": "50d5d1bf-97d2-4d06-8902-e2ffa5c6a73c", "type": "concept", "title": "Demand, demographics, connectivity, tourism policy, pipeline, competition, real estate, labor, infrastructure, and seasonality"}, {"id": "306c90ed-aa5d-4dd5-909f-e46cdd191823", "type": "concept", "title": "Political, regulatory, currency, tax, capital-control, corruption, security, health, climate, and repatriation risk"}, {"id": "0313ecf2-c549-441d-93ce-cb3c47d90564", "type": "concept", "title": "Country, city, resort corridor, micro-location, market timing, data quality, scenario, and risk-adjusted attractiveness"}], "description": "Students compare market potential with institutional, operational, financial, and resilience risks."}, {"id": "4fd0912f-b991-457d-8b1a-027a5eea8065", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Entry modes and international configuration", "children": [{"id": "e926f34e-4fed-406c-bf84-dbdf0218a32a", "type": "concept", "title": "Export/licensing, franchise, master franchise, management contract, lease, acquisition, greenfield, alliance, and joint venture"}, {"id": "de65533e-3125-4b04-96ae-c9b2324a0666", "type": "concept", "title": "Global, multidomestic, transnational, regional, hub-and-spoke, platform, and portfolio configurations"}, {"id": "d972171a-7edb-4e27-9702-2ac1ed101804", "type": "concept", "title": "Partner selection, due diligence, control rights, knowledge transfer, governance, dispute resolution, and exit"}], "description": "This subtopic matches control, commitment, learning, speed, and risk to the expansion vehicle."}, {"id": "b0bfdf47-6780-4e17-adc0-4f12b193f0b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Localization and cross-cultural execution", "children": [{"id": "40ab125d-6260-442c-be3b-e9869460e6a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Brand core, service standards, design, food, language, pricing, marketing, technology, HR, and procurement choices"}, {"id": "4aacb355-a90f-4e39-b287-87a10069025c", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural values, communication, hierarchy, negotiation, service expectations, inclusion, religion, and local authenticity"}, {"id": "5dc9e887-8e2a-4594-8dee-3a6b5ab30ab4", "type": "concept", "title": "Local talent, supplier development, community license, institutional relationships, feedback, and reverse innovation"}], "description": "Students decide what must remain globally consistent and what should adapt to guests, employees, owners, laws, and communities."}]}, {"id": "43ca3410-1a76-4868-baa6-d49ab552625c", "type": "topic", "title": "Financial Strategy, Valuation, Capital Allocation, and Portfolio Performance", "description": "Strategy must translate into cash flows, risk, capital needs, and value. Students learn to connect competitive choices to financial consequences without reducing strategy to a spreadsheet.", "children": [{"id": "7949b61c-d7b5-474d-bac9-b35e42076faf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Strategy-to-finance logic", "children": [{"id": "659c58a6-5f7c-4755-bb92-333075636d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenue growth, rate/mix, occupancy/covers, ancillary spend, retention, channel cost, labor, productivity, margin, and flow-through"}, {"id": "2a5ad9a6-0b56-4427-a0d8-e67ee18ca523", "type": "concept", "title": "Working capital, maintenance capex, growth capex, asset turns, capital intensity, taxes, fees, and financing"}, {"id": "2b40e5ff-043f-4f4c-afa0-2a00bd151037", "type": "concept", "title": "Return on invested capital, cost of capital, economic profit, free cash flow, growth duration, and value creation"}], "description": "Students build a value-driver tree that links strategic choices to demand, pricing, operations, assets, investment, risk, and cash flow."}, {"id": "9db03233-d9a9-4cd4-aa9c-c17fc6fb0234", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Valuation and strategic alternatives", "children": [{"id": "d01a4015-ce25-4d62-b202-446c77232129", "type": "concept", "title": "Incremental cash flows, DCF, terminal value, multiples, precedent transactions, replacement cost, and sum of parts"}, {"id": "e42078d8-ad5c-4c82-9088-5ff0b8423f38", "type": "concept", "title": "Base, upside, downside, stress, break-even, sensitivity, probability, real options, and strategic option value"}, {"id": "93e90a17-d22b-4de9-b175-311459fd92f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Acquire, build, renovate, reposition, franchise, manage, lease, sell, partner, delay, stage, or abandon"}], "description": "This subtopic uses valuation as a disciplined comparison of futures, not a single precise answer."}, {"id": "bbc6092d-06d7-4b19-a2bf-4f45d5c976b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capital allocation and portfolio governance", "children": [{"id": "230f84fa-6717-41bd-a0f9-dbec7d7054b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, expected return, risk, liquidity, capacity, dependencies, timing, optionality, and stakeholder impact"}, {"id": "f273a48c-45c4-4929-a3c2-0c0cf83e07d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Capital rationing, hurdle rates, ranking bias, portfolio balance, debt capacity, dividends, buybacks, and covenant awareness"}, {"id": "d1ba8466-99cd-4554-bb1d-f73735cbf558", "type": "concept", "title": "Approval gates, investment committee, post-audit, benefit realization, reallocation, sunk-cost discipline, and exit"}], "description": "Students allocate scarce capital among properties, brands, technology, people, acquisitions, resilience, and shareholder claims."}]}, {"id": "13868d59-53ba-433c-945f-93fe6dacd0b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Innovation, Entrepreneurship, Digital Platforms, and Responsible AI", "description": "Innovation changes the guest proposition, operating system, business model, or industry architecture. Students learn to search, test, scale, and govern innovation while avoiding technology theater.", "children": [{"id": "6df04af5-9e66-4241-9aef-2669052f0833", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Innovation strategy and ambidexterity", "children": [{"id": "12ff463c-fd67-420d-b6a0-c52fff350e2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Incremental, architectural, radical, disruptive, sustaining, process, experience, business-model, and ecosystem innovation"}, {"id": "f78c3eb6-6f2e-45a2-a7c2-d52f873dbe06", "type": "concept", "title": "Search domains, portfolio thesis, core-adjacent-transformational horizons, ambidexterity, resource separation, and senior sponsorship"}, {"id": "9676d9d0-6f5c-4549-87f1-8bc21ce0de88", "type": "concept", "title": "Idea funnel, evidence gates, experiment velocity, failure learning, scale readiness, and innovation accounting"}], "description": "Students balance exploitation of the current model with exploration of new opportunities."}, {"id": "e7de842c-069c-4ac7-be34-dc6c62ebe435", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Entrepreneurship and venture design", "children": [{"id": "deb9175a-774b-44ab-8725-428b1d0202e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Opportunity recognition, founder-market fit, customer discovery, value proposition, prototype, MVP, and business-model testing"}, {"id": "f1777170-68b6-4577-b64e-1c9f12aeb622", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit economics, cash runway, funding, milestones, partnerships, intellectual property, regulatory pathway, and go-to-market"}, {"id": "f541b016-dc15-463b-9190-5ba825ee6910", "type": "concept", "title": "Pivot, persevere, pause, partner, spin out, acquire, integrate, or shut down"}], "description": "This subtopic treats new ventures as hypotheses about guests, problems, solutions, channels, economics, and defensibility."}, {"id": "6a22abf6-ee0f-425f-870c-dbf4fb10e63c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital, data, platforms, automation, and AI", "children": [{"id": "b0d696cc-c8dc-4019-a901-1e605ca04b6b", "type": "concept", "title": "PMS/POS/CRS/RMS/CRM, cloud, APIs, data platforms, mobile, robotics, IoT, digital identity, and cybersecurity"}, {"id": "a4fd18f3-3313-4340-a7ad-69ad6a4b1d1b", "type": "concept", "title": "Network effects, marketplaces, platform governance, interoperability, switching costs, vendor lock-in, data rights, and build-buy-partner"}, {"id": "2808843f-7eb8-4a59-8e88-5786699498ac", "type": "concept", "title": "AI use cases, accuracy, bias, privacy, explainability, human oversight, hallucination, security, workforce effects, and NIST AI RMF"}], "description": "Students evaluate technology investments by strategic outcome, operating fit, data, adoption, control, and trust."}]}, {"id": "e9f86cb9-0998-4786-a3cb-d22eac262907", "type": "topic", "title": "Organization Design, Leadership, Change, and Strategy Execution", "description": "A strategy has no effect until resources, structures, incentives, capabilities, routines, and leadership behavior make it real. Students design an execution system that can deliver today while adapting tomorrow.", "children": [{"id": "14dc2144-4ac5-42bb-8754-6fd259ae1177", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Organization and operating-model design", "children": [{"id": "121051ee-1938-4d80-be3d-e5a6bea2b586", "type": "concept", "title": "Functional, divisional, geographic, brand, property, matrix, network, platform, and shared-service structures"}, {"id": "ce0e47ae-7621-4ebb-84e9-d0e711b03079", "type": "concept", "title": "Centralize versus decentralize, spans/layers, governance forums, decision rights, interfaces, service levels, and escalation"}, {"id": "9f643211-0cfe-43b1-a77d-7c8ca569ae21", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate center roles, parenting, centers of excellence, field support, owner relations, and accountability"}], "description": "Students align structure and decision rights with strategic priorities, scale, geography, brands, assets, and capabilities."}, {"id": "f90c3790-5de3-45b0-99ce-2e4e2984756e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Strategic leadership and culture", "children": [{"id": "41480512-7f29-4d41-8a1a-c0ac20e11fa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic intent, sensemaking, communication, coalition, role modeling, resource choices, consistency, and humility"}, {"id": "c36f2477-5173-4b2c-a03e-7947ca52349a", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture diagnosis, desired behaviors, systems reinforcement, psychological safety, inclusion, challenge, voice, and accountability"}, {"id": "e3fb0d83-4d9c-41c6-8f5a-bbac339dad97", "type": "concept", "title": "Board, executives, owners, general managers, department leaders, frontline employees, and distributed leadership"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how leaders create direction, alignment, commitment, trust, learning, and ethical boundaries."}, {"id": "b8696733-4b97-4775-b99e-665a371efaaf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Execution, change, and performance management", "children": [{"id": "4aa52806-e18b-4004-886c-d2cbbc60dbab", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategy map, objectives, key results, balanced scorecard, initiative portfolio, RACI, roadmap, and dependency map"}, {"id": "b9680305-b890-4d84-b6e0-7b70256585ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholder analysis, change story, participation, communication, training, resistance, incentives, adoption, and reinforcement"}, {"id": "2dbd7273-b387-491c-9c7b-e1dab9e43b37", "type": "concept", "title": "Leading/lagging indicators, review cadence, variance, benefits, agile adaptation, stop-start-continue decisions, and after-action review"}], "description": "Students translate priorities into initiatives, measures, owners, milestones, learning loops, and corrective action."}]}, {"id": "20691f3f-d15a-47a0-9599-7a16ebaf6d44", "type": "topic", "title": "Risk, Scenarios, Resilience, Cybersecurity, and Crisis Strategy", "description": "Hospitality enterprises face demand shocks, disasters, cyber events, climate hazards, health crises, geopolitical disruption, and operational failures. Students integrate risk and resilience into strategy instead of treating them as separate compliance exercises.", "children": [{"id": "18e5d6a7-45a9-41df-8419-5f44e61c0dec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Enterprise risk and strategic uncertainty", "children": [{"id": "7f5a9eae-1f77-4bfe-9356-4f8e8e56c0de", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic, financial, operational, legal, cyber, privacy, safety, people, third-party, reputational, climate, and geopolitical risk"}, {"id": "1b89f62c-6e7a-47b3-a7d2-504ca1be5ef6", "type": "concept", "title": "Likelihood, impact, velocity, persistence, detectability, correlation, concentration, risk appetite, tolerance, and capacity"}, {"id": "e40c2614-9099-410f-9f6a-e55811715443", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk register, heat map limitations, bow-tie, stress testing, reverse stress, key risk indicators, and assurance"}], "description": "Students identify threats and opportunities, their causes, velocity, interdependencies, ownership, and potential response."}, {"id": "2dd5ad7f-6af8-43f4-8d03-1b2173200fde", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scenario planning and strategic options", "children": [{"id": "447ab074-f823-437c-a405-b5bb951a8df3", "type": "concept", "title": "Critical uncertainties, scenario matrix, narratives, quantitative implications, competitor behavior, stakeholder response, and signposts"}, {"id": "bb009b4f-4d9d-4d1b-92c3-3cb723b945c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Robust actions, contingent actions, no-regret moves, hedges, real options, staged commitments, and trigger points"}, {"id": "43e4f577-e632-43e2-84b1-6f4079614db0", "type": "concept", "title": "Wild cards, black swans, premortem, red team, sensitivity, decision trees, and continuous monitoring"}], "description": "This subtopic develops strategies that remain coherent across plausible futures and preserves choices when uncertainty is high."}, {"id": "9f2bb32e-85dd-4d05-a29a-fda768a7f246", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Resilience and crisis leadership", "children": [{"id": "a3f36c7b-74b9-4645-83a1-a5717b296d17", "type": "concept", "title": "Business impact analysis, critical services, dependencies, redundancy, backup, alternate suppliers, remote capability, and recovery objectives"}, {"id": "8122f640-e05e-4adb-beec-f7c6f2bd8447", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency command, safety, communication, guest care, employee support, cyber response, insurance, documentation, and stakeholder coordination"}, {"id": "c343c6e1-7d68-45ef-ba43-89fa4a74028d", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate adaptation, physical and transition risk, destination resilience, recovery sequencing, after-action learning, and trust repair"}], "description": "Students plan for continuity, adaptation, recovery, and renewal across properties, systems, people, suppliers, and destinations."}]}, {"id": "7cf4e1bc-e0de-404d-a34e-744f64ba092d", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethics, Corporate Governance, Stakeholders, and Sustainable Value", "description": "Hospitality strategy affects guests, employees, owners, communities, suppliers, destinations, and the environment. Students evaluate legitimacy, governance, distributional effects, and long-term value alongside financial performance.", "children": [{"id": "93b4145d-fe4f-41df-b911-f1790e3fb44a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethical strategic decision making", "children": [{"id": "ccbdb78b-4450-4665-8064-097df7ee63f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal minimum versus ethical responsibility, fiduciary duties, professional judgment, conflicts of interest, and speaking up"}, {"id": "7e72e317-d9c6-4c43-9652-28a4b9634a22", "type": "concept", "title": "Guest safety, labor practices, accessibility, discrimination, pricing fairness, privacy, surveillance, corruption, and human rights"}, {"id": "8ce8339e-27a4-410c-abe2-955484c22234", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholder mapping, options, harms/benefits, reversibility, precedent, transparency, challenge, escalation, and documentation"}], "description": "Students make values, duties, consequences, rights, justice, conflicts, and affected stakeholders explicit."}, {"id": "d08130d1-1f3d-4283-b23e-1455d0a92565", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Boards, owners, controls, and governance", "children": [{"id": "05db5053-83a8-4751-ae17-c467d421065a", "type": "concept", "title": "Board roles, independence, committees, executive authority, owner representation, franchise councils, and stakeholder forums"}, {"id": "8a853fac-5bd4-4aec-a4ea-5c9a96788026", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategy oversight, risk, succession, compensation, audit/internal control, cyber, data, AI, sustainability, and crisis governance"}, {"id": "9bae8974-5eb5-4e1f-9f6b-54d0d18278f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Information asymmetry, agency problems, related parties, incentives, misconduct, whistleblowing, assurance, and board evaluation"}], "description": "This subtopic clarifies oversight, accountability, incentives, information quality, and checks on managerial power."}, {"id": "6fe5eb08-18c6-4f88-a743-d1db0a271495", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sustainability, impact, and shared value", "children": [{"id": "96ca8fdf-4760-42a4-90cc-e295b624133f", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy, water, waste, food loss, carbon, biodiversity, sourcing, labor, community, housing, overtourism, and cultural heritage"}, {"id": "12a07583-088e-4368-92b8-d47f6ab39351", "type": "concept", "title": "Materiality, boundaries, baselines, targets, transition plans, life-cycle cost, circularity, resilience, and credible claims"}, {"id": "45f4be70-abff-434e-8c7c-6db082d4a07a", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholder outcomes, tradeoffs, distributional impact, greenwashing, measurement, assurance, policy, and value creation"}], "description": "Students integrate environmental and social dependencies into business-model, investment, supply-chain, and destination choices."}]}, {"id": "e30ca45a-df7b-4102-803c-ed4e01ce4f3d", "type": "topic", "title": "Integrative Hospitality Strategy Case and Board-Level Communication", "description": "The capstone requires students to diagnose a real or realistic enterprise, choose among credible alternatives, quantify consequences, design execution, and defend the recommendation under challenge.", "children": [{"id": "943d1f98-a833-469f-8bd5-b695f8097253", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case analysis and evidence synthesis", "children": [{"id": "a7a19cf0-90a3-4e99-9d4e-250c3e16a9a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Case facts, timeline, stakeholders, financial/operating data, market evidence, contracts, organization, and uncertainties"}, {"id": "dc3ee1d1-caa2-4add-95bb-8790e28288c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Issue tree, external/internal synthesis, business model, advantage, value drivers, risks, ethical dimensions, and assumptions"}, {"id": "cefaee6d-f845-43e2-b6b2-cf3694602358", "type": "concept", "title": "Materiality, triangulation, contradictory evidence, missing information, sensitivity, and confidence"}], "description": "Students move from a large evidence set to the few material issues that determine the decision."}, {"id": "909930bb-5c27-47dc-bf9f-3b0cc4896b0d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Alternatives and recommendation", "children": [{"id": "e0473439-f9aa-4f23-997a-46b1e40f8efc", "type": "concept", "title": "Status quo, improve, focus, grow, partner, acquire, divest, transform, stage, and exit alternatives"}, {"id": "c76bfd7b-8e33-4251-8891-fdd4da33b7d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, advantage, value, feasibility, capital, risk, resilience, ethics, stakeholder impact, timing, and reversibility"}, {"id": "07d99724-0ebb-472c-9b75-a3dc40e75d04", "type": "concept", "title": "Recommendation, rationale, rejected alternatives, conditions, milestones, contingencies, triggers, and kill criteria"}], "description": "This subtopic requires genuine options, explicit criteria, comparable economics, stakeholder consequences, and a coherent choice."}, {"id": "dce74450-4485-401d-a99d-7082527dcdd6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Board memo, presentation, and defense", "children": [{"id": "fa47e2a8-155b-4ece-9924-b1a34dc5baf6", "type": "concept", "title": "Executive summary, decision request, key exhibits, financial bridge, scenario table, implementation roadmap, and risk register"}, {"id": "7e0774e8-66cc-4ec8-a817-e7164a2c9bf1", "type": "concept", "title": "Pyramid structure, assertion-evidence slides, source notes, accessible visuals, assumptions, limitations, and appendix"}, {"id": "0757024d-527c-4151-aea9-c71fc870fe5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral briefing, team roles, Q&A, challenge response, negotiation, revision, decision log, and reflection"}], "description": "Students communicate with clarity, brevity, evidence, and readiness for skeptical questions."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$hstmjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'hospitality-strategic-management'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Hospitality Strategic Management',
  goal = 'Hospitality Strategic Management',
  summary = 'This senior capstone integrates hospitality marketing, finance, accounting, operations, people, technology, real estate, law, and communication. Students diagnose industries and enterprises, evaluate resources and business models, choose competitive and corporate strategies, assess ownership and global-growth options, connect strategy to valuation and capital allocation, govern innovation and AI, design resilient and sustainable operating models, and lead implementation. A case-based strategy challenge culminates in a board-ready memo, analytical exhibits, presentation, and defense.',
  data = $hstmjson${"slug": "hospitality-strategic-management", "title": "Hospitality Strategic Management", "description": "This senior capstone integrates hospitality marketing, finance, accounting, operations, people, technology, real estate, law, and communication. Students diagnose industries and enterprises, evaluate resources and business models, choose competitive and corporate strategies, assess ownership and global-growth options, connect strategy to valuation and capital allocation, govern innovation and AI, design resilient and sustainable operating models, and lead implementation. A case-based strategy challenge culminates in a board-ready memo, analytical exhibits, presentation, and defense.", "topics": [{"id": "6505fb8a-c88c-4232-b41b-5c8c28f1e37d", "type": "topic", "title": "Strategic Management, Integrative Thinking, and Strategic Diagnosis", "description": "Strategic management asks how a hospitality enterprise can create durable value when guests, competitors, assets, talent, technology, capital, and institutions are changing. This opening section establishes a disciplined process for diagnosis, choice, execution, and learning.", "children": [{"id": "f5c7de7f-481d-4368-8770-b2bc707b1763", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The work of strategy", "children": [{"id": "4c1fe305-afa1-4d74-a714-ad35fbdb0586", "type": "concept", "title": "Mission, purpose, vision, values, objectives, strategy, policy, tactics, operating plan, and business model"}, {"id": "d29c2a50-8919-471f-914e-2e8763d287ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent action, choice, tradeoff, fit, positioning, advantage, and renewal"}, {"id": "c7b6bec9-5b53-4be2-887b-2f73e44beda4", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate, business-unit, brand, property, function, platform, destination, and ecosystem levels"}], "description": "Students distinguish strategy from goals, budgets, operational excellence, slogans, and isolated initiatives."}, {"id": "92c96383-1c8a-439b-a23d-1149cb6dba8e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Strategic problem framing", "children": [{"id": "2d1c413a-3186-4883-8632-50105754358f", "type": "concept", "title": "Symptoms versus causes, issue trees, hypotheses, assumptions, constraints, uncertainties, and decision rights"}, {"id": "f8d840ca-d8f1-464a-b66d-147619aaaae7", "type": "concept", "title": "Relevant time horizon, unit of analysis, stakeholder perspectives, boundaries, counterfactual, and opportunity cost"}, {"id": "6539d3e7-80ac-4315-bb45-7b0b07f585d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Facts, estimates, forecasts, scenarios, inference, judgment, bias, and confidence"}], "description": "This subtopic turns an ambiguous management situation into the key question, decision, evidence needs, alternatives, and criteria."}, {"id": "d6f91b9f-60cd-46d9-8dff-e5d8d5c46afc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Integrated hospitality diagnosis", "children": [{"id": "78459be8-449f-4846-981f-f99a026da493", "type": "concept", "title": "Guest promise, service system, demand, capacity, channel, labor, asset, capital, owner, and community linkages"}, {"id": "596991e1-7e0c-486c-9d6f-c51c4df8a039", "type": "concept", "title": "Key performance drivers, reinforcing loops, bottlenecks, tensions, second-order effects, and unintended consequences"}, {"id": "beab762e-dd36-4a53-bbd4-e3692e0cbb47", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic narrative, evidence table, material issues, priorities, and concise executive problem statement"}], "description": "Students connect marketing, operations, people, technology, finance, real estate, law, and sustainability into one enterprise view."}]}, {"id": "5c9ea195-3358-48b1-955a-02bb4c7987aa", "type": "topic", "title": "External Environment, Industry Structure, and Hospitality Ecosystems", "description": "No firm chooses strategy in a vacuum. Students assess macro forces, industry economics, competitors, complementors, platforms, regulators, and local stakeholders to identify structural opportunities and threats.", "children": [{"id": "c990ae6f-5378-4f6d-bcbe-2a537fc5ee98", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Macroenvironment and scenarios", "children": [{"id": "f8f56e67-903f-4682-833f-894de285733d", "type": "concept", "title": "Political, economic, social, technological, environmental, and legal analysis"}, {"id": "ca3b7937-fe1b-4bef-b0b4-2f77047e2fa7", "type": "concept", "title": "Demographics, mobility, work patterns, inflation, labor, interest rates, health, climate, geopolitics, and regulation"}, {"id": "5efc4354-11ac-428b-bdd5-0ca8d3530efc", "type": "concept", "title": "Predetermined elements, critical uncertainties, weak signals, scenario axes, narratives, implications, and signposts"}], "description": "Students examine forces that can reshape demand, cost, access, investment, and legitimacy."}, {"id": "d64518f2-a873-4fcf-9d5b-75b2fc93e784", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Industry structure and profit pools", "children": [{"id": "4188a654-05fb-40f9-8937-75af91e85693", "type": "concept", "title": "Five forces, market definition, strategic groups, concentration, entry barriers, switching costs, and exit barriers"}, {"id": "3ecaa984-f76d-499f-9ecc-730460b47c72", "type": "concept", "title": "Brand, owner, operator, franchisee, landlord, lender, OTA, GDS, supplier, employee, and guest bargaining power"}, {"id": "9dc5858a-200d-49ff-a244-23be9a2e6676", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenue pools, cost pools, margin pools, channel leakage, asset intensity, cycle, maturity, and structural change"}], "description": "This subtopic explains how bargaining power, rivalry, entry, substitutes, complements, and capacity shape value capture."}, {"id": "b9d89191-c4f4-4dab-8ebc-02990332564c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Competitors, platforms, and ecosystems", "children": [{"id": "19466fa4-e6fd-428c-b6d8-cedac480f06c", "type": "concept", "title": "Competitive set, strategic-group map, benchmarking, relative cost, differentiation, moves, countermoves, and game theory"}, {"id": "9caeed64-b88a-47e9-b2c8-0a5ebf99ff65", "type": "concept", "title": "Airlines, destinations, attractions, mobility, payment, loyalty, technology, media, food delivery, and experience partners"}, {"id": "db65d5ba-67b2-4825-b481-56e228539be0", "type": "concept", "title": "Network effects, standards, data access, multihoming, orchestration, complementors, coopetition, and ecosystem health"}], "description": "Students analyze rivalry and collaboration across interconnected hospitality and travel systems."}]}, {"id": "b4bacfc9-3e5a-457d-a8e0-3d84f7a625b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Resources, Capabilities, Value Chains, and Organizational Advantage", "description": "External opportunity matters only when the organization can act. Students identify the tangible, intangible, human, relational, and organizational resources that produce distinctive guest and financial outcomes.", "children": [{"id": "77f1f73f-670a-47b0-ab90-d93981b34370", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Resource and capability analysis", "children": [{"id": "ee8d759c-f9d3-416b-88d8-12233080adc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Physical assets, locations, capital, data, technology, brands, contracts, intellectual property, culture, talent, and relationships"}, {"id": "c1a06fbf-da54-426e-82b9-3298d846e530", "type": "concept", "title": "Resource versus capability, routines, systems, dynamic capabilities, absorptive capacity, learning, and path dependence"}, {"id": "99492fba-a38b-4898-9346-860c4046cb23", "type": "concept", "title": "Value, rarity, imitability, organization, durability, transferability, substitutability, and appropriation"}], "description": "Students evaluate what the enterprise owns, accesses, coordinates, learns, and protects."}, {"id": "7a6bdff8-08b7-4534-b5de-f630b64223f8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Value chain and activity-system analysis", "children": [{"id": "baf1096a-ed7c-4eaf-a846-701dac0c3536", "type": "concept", "title": "Development, procurement, distribution, marketing, booking, arrival, operations, service, recovery, loyalty, and support activities"}, {"id": "e5d5ef2f-14c2-46e1-8bf3-27cedb4f655d", "type": "concept", "title": "Outsource versus own, shared services, clusters, centralization, local adaptation, interfaces, handoffs, and bottlenecks"}, {"id": "0e2b32a7-9518-40d9-9908-58c76119eb15", "type": "concept", "title": "Activity fit, complementarities, tradeoffs, cost drivers, willingness-to-pay drivers, leakage, and redesign"}], "description": "This subtopic maps how activities combine to deliver the guest promise and where cost, quality, speed, risk, and differentiation arise."}, {"id": "29c08560-35ac-40b1-be00-65c99cf91296", "type": "subtopic", "title": "People, culture, and relational capability", "children": [{"id": "159ed741-16ad-4e38-9478-a6a052277569", "type": "concept", "title": "Leadership, psychological safety, employee value proposition, skills, staffing model, incentives, voice, and retention"}, {"id": "29a0a352-263c-49c3-a8ab-f95e01f4e4af", "type": "concept", "title": "Service climate, norms, stories, rituals, identity, inclusion, accountability, learning, and ethical culture"}, {"id": "b21fe87c-7c5f-4ce5-a427-41e1fe7df5c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Owner-operator, brand-franchisee, labor, vendor, community, government, and partner relationships"}], "description": "Students treat service culture and stakeholder relationships as strategic systems rather than soft background conditions."}]}, {"id": "1091e6a2-2f51-4761-8937-65c5420e7af5", "type": "topic", "title": "Business Models, Competitive Positioning, and Advantage", "description": "A business model explains who receives value, how it is delivered, and how the enterprise captures enough value to sustain the system. Students compare alternative positions and test whether choices reinforce one another.", "children": [{"id": "92826e62-89d4-4ddc-b92b-2d14322ac94f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hospitality business-model design", "children": [{"id": "4bd2a230-abac-44a1-9abc-352ff54e83f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Target guests, jobs-to-be-done, value proposition, channels, relationships, key activities, resources, and partners"}, {"id": "57fb6e89-ff7a-48a2-b293-c0cb6faa2442", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenue model, pricing, unit economics, cost structure, working capital, asset intensity, scalability, and risk allocation"}, {"id": "8752124d-60d8-4b2a-ad4b-9bee57736d3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Owned, leased, franchised, managed, licensed, membership, subscription, marketplace, aggregator, and hybrid models"}], "description": "Students specify the customer, proposition, activities, resources, partners, economics, and governance of a hospitality concept."}, {"id": "8adfe6e6-7c60-46fd-bbd6-4b849a37ee05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cost leadership, differentiation, and focus", "children": [{"id": "cd148401-4c43-4dab-8b62-bf1c885a1b74", "type": "concept", "title": "Relative cost, economies of scale/scope/density/learning, capacity utilization, process design, and procurement"}, {"id": "5a8316e2-58bf-4fb6-953f-0446105e973b", "type": "concept", "title": "Brand meaning, experience, design, convenience, personalization, trust, location, community, quality, and innovation"}, {"id": "8812acc6-8139-40b3-aa65-3e241a39b341", "type": "concept", "title": "Broad versus focused scope, best-cost positions, strategic ambiguity, imitation, erosion, and being stuck in the middle"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how firms create superior value through a deliberately configured activity system."}, {"id": "12adca9a-cf94-44dc-b077-9cb30d7fb81f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Value innovation and strategy testing", "children": [{"id": "17e4e41f-2862-4b28-8c6f-8d53f6824aed", "type": "concept", "title": "Value curves, eliminate-reduce-raise-create, noncustomers, substitutes, complements, and pain points"}, {"id": "72992ecf-6ead-4206-a875-450d5d266485", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, tradeoffs, consistency, reinforcing activities, isolating mechanisms, and response scenarios"}, {"id": "998cab54-2f11-47da-ba96-b18aa9cd94bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept tests, pilots, minimum viable business model, leading indicators, kill criteria, and learning agenda"}], "description": "Students challenge inherited industry assumptions and test whether a proposed position is coherent, desirable, feasible, viable, and defensible."}]}, {"id": "ee1943c9-3196-4da7-b214-95503370572c", "type": "topic", "title": "Growth, Diversification, Vertical Scope, M&A, and Alliances", "description": "Growth creates value only when the firm has an advantage in the new activity and the benefits exceed complexity, capital, and execution risk. Students evaluate organic and inorganic pathways with explicit parenting logic.", "children": [{"id": "dd9e6042-a591-4aed-a65b-0d63a35f4de2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Organic growth and portfolio pathways", "children": [{"id": "070e1974-2a3d-4f46-aed2-55f630841d42", "type": "concept", "title": "Market penetration, product development, market development, adjacency, replication, conversion, and new concept creation"}, {"id": "2db19938-1c58-4401-a425-6f1ddc167736", "type": "concept", "title": "Same-store growth, new units, white space, pipeline, ramp-up, cannibalization, saturation, and learning curve"}, {"id": "a466b9b9-f0c3-41dd-9736-dfca92ddda74", "type": "concept", "title": "Core, adjacent, transformational horizons; options, sequencing, stage gates, and resource commitments"}], "description": "Students compare deeper penetration, new segments, new markets, new offers, and adjacent services."}, {"id": "c8b647a1-6a16-4fe5-a542-4950a3070cb1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diversification and vertical integration", "children": [{"id": "49dbfabe-8d21-4984-8803-6314b724c934", "type": "concept", "title": "Related and unrelated diversification, economies of scope, shared activities, cross-selling, brand leverage, and internal capital markets"}, {"id": "1139de4d-0c79-43da-a390-44e96d6cbdff", "type": "concept", "title": "Backward and forward integration, transaction costs, hold-up, quality control, data, coordination, flexibility, and antitrust awareness"}, {"id": "c7194708-c56b-48bf-b209-934a90dea534", "type": "concept", "title": "Parenting advantage, better-off test, ownership test, complexity discount, bureaucracy, and divestiture"}], "description": "This subtopic tests when corporate ownership creates more value than market contracting."}, {"id": "d7a58011-8250-4fce-9348-f4acbe6344d5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mergers, acquisitions, joint ventures, and alliances", "children": [{"id": "b51ae0e5-f7a7-4e07-b948-dc05751bd4c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Build, buy, partner, license, franchise, joint venture, minority investment, management agreement, and strategic alliance"}, {"id": "db37cb37-99ca-40f2-9935-7d5dd0b5ba50", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, standalone value, synergies, control premium, diligence, financing, conditions, and downside case"}, {"id": "4bd42a8b-855a-48f4-a284-c991d73fd46f", "type": "concept", "title": "Integration thesis, culture, systems, talent, brand, customers, Day 1, milestones, synergy tracking, and exit"}], "description": "Students examine target logic, valuation discipline, deal structure, integration, and post-deal accountability."}]}, {"id": "73886c80-e5d0-4f42-87b8-c2eacd789cb6", "type": "topic", "title": "Brand Portfolio, Customer, Channel, and Commercial Strategy", "description": "Hospitality enterprises compete through portfolios of brands, relationships, channels, and revenue streams. Students align customer value and commercial systems with corporate strategy and property economics.", "children": [{"id": "cbf345e1-a317-4d03-a7a7-191dfb5e1fc0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Brand architecture and portfolio roles", "children": [{"id": "b6a0bf9f-35f8-4e4a-93c8-86cca2a470dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Branded house, house of brands, endorsed brands, soft brands, collections, flags, outlet brands, and co-brands"}, {"id": "1436db84-2478-4295-93a6-37c8341879aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Target, promise, price tier, occasion, geography, owner proposition, standards, and portfolio role"}, {"id": "675fe3db-74d0-4406-94d4-4a3fa7540f99", "type": "concept", "title": "Gap, overlap, cannibalization, extension, conversion, revitalization, retirement, and portfolio governance"}], "description": "Students decide how brands cover segments without creating costly overlap or confusing guests, owners, and employees."}, {"id": "bd686bb6-cc23-42ba-9b3c-9e5d25f3c954", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Customer and channel strategy", "children": [{"id": "975b4a66-0113-4aaa-9c0c-6a96cd29ffc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Segments, corporate accounts, groups, members, locals, residents, intermediaries, and lifetime value"}, {"id": "0f3cc29b-9e56-49de-be1a-3cec4e682d7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Direct, brand, OTA, GDS, wholesale, metasearch, delivery, membership, partnership, and loyalty channels"}, {"id": "664c9e63-7ea5-419e-bff5-8c38cd10adab", "type": "concept", "title": "Acquisition cost, commission, contribution, data access, retention, bargaining power, channel conflict, and incrementality"}], "description": "This subtopic evaluates which demand to attract, how to reach it, and what relationships are worth building."}, {"id": "0648d11c-4d7d-4dc3-8129-5896f501113d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Commercial operating model", "children": [{"id": "c6f18812-5d04-4e8b-b156-b9bdf0246d12", "type": "concept", "title": "Demand generation, pricing, inventory, group displacement, promotions, partnerships, and ancillary revenue"}, {"id": "35e55bcb-95ec-4a74-b284-aa2a3ed33c7b", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial organization, decision rights, shared metrics, incentives, meeting cadence, forecast, and accountability"}, {"id": "7ca3e050-f401-481d-8150-fea6b3411ba2", "type": "concept", "title": "Top-line growth versus profitable demand, brand equity, guest trust, operational readiness, and owner return"}], "description": "Students integrate marketing, sales, revenue management, distribution, CRM, and operations around shared enterprise goals."}]}, {"id": "f8b9621f-9153-4a96-b002-abdbfc7009a7", "type": "topic", "title": "Ownership, Asset-Light Strategy, Contracts, and Hotel Asset Management", "description": "Hospitality strategy often separates real-estate ownership, brand, operations, distribution, and capital. Students learn how contracts allocate economics, control, risk, and incentives across the owner-brand-operator system.", "children": [{"id": "9ac82949-8707-471b-a974-f2f525445147", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ownership and operating structures", "children": [{"id": "17c43807-6f00-4737-a6bf-00492d790270", "type": "concept", "title": "Hotel owner, operator, brand, franchisee, franchisor, landlord, tenant, lender, asset manager, and third-party manager roles"}, {"id": "a6534e7a-cb6a-4510-8b84-2e4ebfe60b60", "type": "concept", "title": "Control, capital intensity, fee streams, residual claims, guarantees, key money, territorial rights, and risk sharing"}, {"id": "5df5b04f-c423-4db9-917d-b01755f579de", "type": "concept", "title": "Management agreements, franchise agreements, leases, licenses, SLAs, covenants, and termination rights"}], "description": "Students compare owned, leased, managed, franchised, licensed, and affiliated arrangements."}, {"id": "ecbe0f43-42bc-4478-8d87-2731cd2915c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asset-light and capital-light strategy", "children": [{"id": "57647c33-f929-4c87-b5a3-7a7e7fe660f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Fee growth, return on invested capital, scalability, volatility, balance-sheet capacity, brand reach, and pipeline"}, {"id": "9fbc7434-239c-4fda-ad21-8f8d6dbfc41d", "type": "concept", "title": "Quality-control loss, owner dependence, contract rigidity, fee conflicts, capital support, reputational spillovers, and governance cost"}, {"id": "0b35e8d2-3a03-499f-a87e-6a439a1fc662", "type": "concept", "title": "Owned-versus-managed/franchised mix, master franchising, development partners, conversions, and platform economics"}], "description": "This subtopic explains why firms separate assets from operations and when that separation creates or destroys value."}, {"id": "5c1c5c3d-6b0e-45cd-886a-3bfe8e72f7de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asset-management and owner strategy", "children": [{"id": "55e022f0-e259-48c7-8f70-7e8a568a579a", "type": "concept", "title": "Market position, GOP, EBITDA-related measures, cash flow, reserve, debt, valuation, cap rate, and total return"}, {"id": "01644566-66be-432f-8e51-707b3a48bdb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Budget challenge, benchmarking, flow-through, labor, channel cost, capex, PIP, deferred maintenance, and sustainability investment"}, {"id": "e26a53b9-9a1b-4131-9792-8938479a448e", "type": "concept", "title": "Hold, sell, refinance, renovate, reposition, rebrand, convert, change operator, adaptive reuse, and exit timing"}], "description": "Students evaluate property performance, capital needs, brand/operator fit, and hold-sell-renovate-reposition decisions."}]}, {"id": "bb83908c-d224-4242-84b5-10d27880b127", "type": "topic", "title": "Global Expansion, Institutions, Culture, and Localization", "description": "International growth exposes firms to new demand and resources while increasing distance, institutional complexity, political risk, and cultural adaptation needs. Students learn to choose where, how, and at what pace to expand.", "children": [{"id": "2fe14201-0a3f-45f8-8400-434f4a1b1dd9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Country and city attractiveness", "children": [{"id": "50d5d1bf-97d2-4d06-8902-e2ffa5c6a73c", "type": "concept", "title": "Demand, demographics, connectivity, tourism policy, pipeline, competition, real estate, labor, infrastructure, and seasonality"}, {"id": "306c90ed-aa5d-4dd5-909f-e46cdd191823", "type": "concept", "title": "Political, regulatory, currency, tax, capital-control, corruption, security, health, climate, and repatriation risk"}, {"id": "0313ecf2-c549-441d-93ce-cb3c47d90564", "type": "concept", "title": "Country, city, resort corridor, micro-location, market timing, data quality, scenario, and risk-adjusted attractiveness"}], "description": "Students compare market potential with institutional, operational, financial, and resilience risks."}, {"id": "4fd0912f-b991-457d-8b1a-027a5eea8065", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Entry modes and international configuration", "children": [{"id": "e926f34e-4fed-406c-bf84-dbdf0218a32a", "type": "concept", "title": "Export/licensing, franchise, master franchise, management contract, lease, acquisition, greenfield, alliance, and joint venture"}, {"id": "de65533e-3125-4b04-96ae-c9b2324a0666", "type": "concept", "title": "Global, multidomestic, transnational, regional, hub-and-spoke, platform, and portfolio configurations"}, {"id": "d972171a-7edb-4e27-9702-2ac1ed101804", "type": "concept", "title": "Partner selection, due diligence, control rights, knowledge transfer, governance, dispute resolution, and exit"}], "description": "This subtopic matches control, commitment, learning, speed, and risk to the expansion vehicle."}, {"id": "b0bfdf47-6780-4e17-adc0-4f12b193f0b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Localization and cross-cultural execution", "children": [{"id": "40ab125d-6260-442c-be3b-e9869460e6a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Brand core, service standards, design, food, language, pricing, marketing, technology, HR, and procurement choices"}, {"id": "4aacb355-a90f-4e39-b287-87a10069025c", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural values, communication, hierarchy, negotiation, service expectations, inclusion, religion, and local authenticity"}, {"id": "5dc9e887-8e2a-4594-8dee-3a6b5ab30ab4", "type": "concept", "title": "Local talent, supplier development, community license, institutional relationships, feedback, and reverse innovation"}], "description": "Students decide what must remain globally consistent and what should adapt to guests, employees, owners, laws, and communities."}]}, {"id": "43ca3410-1a76-4868-baa6-d49ab552625c", "type": "topic", "title": "Financial Strategy, Valuation, Capital Allocation, and Portfolio Performance", "description": "Strategy must translate into cash flows, risk, capital needs, and value. Students learn to connect competitive choices to financial consequences without reducing strategy to a spreadsheet.", "children": [{"id": "7949b61c-d7b5-474d-bac9-b35e42076faf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Strategy-to-finance logic", "children": [{"id": "659c58a6-5f7c-4755-bb92-333075636d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenue growth, rate/mix, occupancy/covers, ancillary spend, retention, channel cost, labor, productivity, margin, and flow-through"}, {"id": "2a5ad9a6-0b56-4427-a0d8-e67ee18ca523", "type": "concept", "title": "Working capital, maintenance capex, growth capex, asset turns, capital intensity, taxes, fees, and financing"}, {"id": "2b40e5ff-043f-4f4c-afa0-2a00bd151037", "type": "concept", "title": "Return on invested capital, cost of capital, economic profit, free cash flow, growth duration, and value creation"}], "description": "Students build a value-driver tree that links strategic choices to demand, pricing, operations, assets, investment, risk, and cash flow."}, {"id": "9db03233-d9a9-4cd4-aa9c-c17fc6fb0234", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Valuation and strategic alternatives", "children": [{"id": "d01a4015-ce25-4d62-b202-446c77232129", "type": "concept", "title": "Incremental cash flows, DCF, terminal value, multiples, precedent transactions, replacement cost, and sum of parts"}, {"id": "e42078d8-ad5c-4c82-9088-5ff0b8423f38", "type": "concept", "title": "Base, upside, downside, stress, break-even, sensitivity, probability, real options, and strategic option value"}, {"id": "93e90a17-d22b-4de9-b175-311459fd92f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Acquire, build, renovate, reposition, franchise, manage, lease, sell, partner, delay, stage, or abandon"}], "description": "This subtopic uses valuation as a disciplined comparison of futures, not a single precise answer."}, {"id": "bbc6092d-06d7-4b19-a2bf-4f45d5c976b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capital allocation and portfolio governance", "children": [{"id": "230f84fa-6717-41bd-a0f9-dbec7d7054b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, expected return, risk, liquidity, capacity, dependencies, timing, optionality, and stakeholder impact"}, {"id": "f273a48c-45c4-4929-a3c2-0c0cf83e07d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Capital rationing, hurdle rates, ranking bias, portfolio balance, debt capacity, dividends, buybacks, and covenant awareness"}, {"id": "d1ba8466-99cd-4554-bb1d-f73735cbf558", "type": "concept", "title": "Approval gates, investment committee, post-audit, benefit realization, reallocation, sunk-cost discipline, and exit"}], "description": "Students allocate scarce capital among properties, brands, technology, people, acquisitions, resilience, and shareholder claims."}]}, {"id": "13868d59-53ba-433c-945f-93fe6dacd0b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Innovation, Entrepreneurship, Digital Platforms, and Responsible AI", "description": "Innovation changes the guest proposition, operating system, business model, or industry architecture. Students learn to search, test, scale, and govern innovation while avoiding technology theater.", "children": [{"id": "6df04af5-9e66-4241-9aef-2669052f0833", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Innovation strategy and ambidexterity", "children": [{"id": "12ff463c-fd67-420d-b6a0-c52fff350e2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Incremental, architectural, radical, disruptive, sustaining, process, experience, business-model, and ecosystem innovation"}, {"id": "f78c3eb6-6f2e-45a2-a7c2-d52f873dbe06", "type": "concept", "title": "Search domains, portfolio thesis, core-adjacent-transformational horizons, ambidexterity, resource separation, and senior sponsorship"}, {"id": "9676d9d0-6f5c-4549-87f1-8bc21ce0de88", "type": "concept", "title": "Idea funnel, evidence gates, experiment velocity, failure learning, scale readiness, and innovation accounting"}], "description": "Students balance exploitation of the current model with exploration of new opportunities."}, {"id": "e7de842c-069c-4ac7-be34-dc6c62ebe435", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Entrepreneurship and venture design", "children": [{"id": "deb9175a-774b-44ab-8725-428b1d0202e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Opportunity recognition, founder-market fit, customer discovery, value proposition, prototype, MVP, and business-model testing"}, {"id": "f1777170-68b6-4577-b64e-1c9f12aeb622", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit economics, cash runway, funding, milestones, partnerships, intellectual property, regulatory pathway, and go-to-market"}, {"id": "f541b016-dc15-463b-9190-5ba825ee6910", "type": "concept", "title": "Pivot, persevere, pause, partner, spin out, acquire, integrate, or shut down"}], "description": "This subtopic treats new ventures as hypotheses about guests, problems, solutions, channels, economics, and defensibility."}, {"id": "6a22abf6-ee0f-425f-870c-dbf4fb10e63c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital, data, platforms, automation, and AI", "children": [{"id": "b0d696cc-c8dc-4019-a901-1e605ca04b6b", "type": "concept", "title": "PMS/POS/CRS/RMS/CRM, cloud, APIs, data platforms, mobile, robotics, IoT, digital identity, and cybersecurity"}, {"id": "a4fd18f3-3313-4340-a7ad-69ad6a4b1d1b", "type": "concept", "title": "Network effects, marketplaces, platform governance, interoperability, switching costs, vendor lock-in, data rights, and build-buy-partner"}, {"id": "2808843f-7eb8-4a59-8e88-5786699498ac", "type": "concept", "title": "AI use cases, accuracy, bias, privacy, explainability, human oversight, hallucination, security, workforce effects, and NIST AI RMF"}], "description": "Students evaluate technology investments by strategic outcome, operating fit, data, adoption, control, and trust."}]}, {"id": "e9f86cb9-0998-4786-a3cb-d22eac262907", "type": "topic", "title": "Organization Design, Leadership, Change, and Strategy Execution", "description": "A strategy has no effect until resources, structures, incentives, capabilities, routines, and leadership behavior make it real. Students design an execution system that can deliver today while adapting tomorrow.", "children": [{"id": "14dc2144-4ac5-42bb-8754-6fd259ae1177", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Organization and operating-model design", "children": [{"id": "121051ee-1938-4d80-be3d-e5a6bea2b586", "type": "concept", "title": "Functional, divisional, geographic, brand, property, matrix, network, platform, and shared-service structures"}, {"id": "ce0e47ae-7621-4ebb-84e9-d0e711b03079", "type": "concept", "title": "Centralize versus decentralize, spans/layers, governance forums, decision rights, interfaces, service levels, and escalation"}, {"id": "9f643211-0cfe-43b1-a77d-7c8ca569ae21", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate center roles, parenting, centers of excellence, field support, owner relations, and accountability"}], "description": "Students align structure and decision rights with strategic priorities, scale, geography, brands, assets, and capabilities."}, {"id": "f90c3790-5de3-45b0-99ce-2e4e2984756e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Strategic leadership and culture", "children": [{"id": "41480512-7f29-4d41-8a1a-c0ac20e11fa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic intent, sensemaking, communication, coalition, role modeling, resource choices, consistency, and humility"}, {"id": "c36f2477-5173-4b2c-a03e-7947ca52349a", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture diagnosis, desired behaviors, systems reinforcement, psychological safety, inclusion, challenge, voice, and accountability"}, {"id": "e3fb0d83-4d9c-41c6-8f5a-bbac339dad97", "type": "concept", "title": "Board, executives, owners, general managers, department leaders, frontline employees, and distributed leadership"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how leaders create direction, alignment, commitment, trust, learning, and ethical boundaries."}, {"id": "b8696733-4b97-4775-b99e-665a371efaaf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Execution, change, and performance management", "children": [{"id": "4aa52806-e18b-4004-886c-d2cbbc60dbab", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategy map, objectives, key results, balanced scorecard, initiative portfolio, RACI, roadmap, and dependency map"}, {"id": "b9680305-b890-4d84-b6e0-7b70256585ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholder analysis, change story, participation, communication, training, resistance, incentives, adoption, and reinforcement"}, {"id": "2dbd7273-b387-491c-9c7b-e1dab9e43b37", "type": "concept", "title": "Leading/lagging indicators, review cadence, variance, benefits, agile adaptation, stop-start-continue decisions, and after-action review"}], "description": "Students translate priorities into initiatives, measures, owners, milestones, learning loops, and corrective action."}]}, {"id": "20691f3f-d15a-47a0-9599-7a16ebaf6d44", "type": "topic", "title": "Risk, Scenarios, Resilience, Cybersecurity, and Crisis Strategy", "description": "Hospitality enterprises face demand shocks, disasters, cyber events, climate hazards, health crises, geopolitical disruption, and operational failures. Students integrate risk and resilience into strategy instead of treating them as separate compliance exercises.", "children": [{"id": "18e5d6a7-45a9-41df-8419-5f44e61c0dec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Enterprise risk and strategic uncertainty", "children": [{"id": "7f5a9eae-1f77-4bfe-9356-4f8e8e56c0de", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic, financial, operational, legal, cyber, privacy, safety, people, third-party, reputational, climate, and geopolitical risk"}, {"id": "1b89f62c-6e7a-47b3-a7d2-504ca1be5ef6", "type": "concept", "title": "Likelihood, impact, velocity, persistence, detectability, correlation, concentration, risk appetite, tolerance, and capacity"}, {"id": "e40c2614-9099-410f-9f6a-e55811715443", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk register, heat map limitations, bow-tie, stress testing, reverse stress, key risk indicators, and assurance"}], "description": "Students identify threats and opportunities, their causes, velocity, interdependencies, ownership, and potential response."}, {"id": "2dd5ad7f-6af8-43f4-8d03-1b2173200fde", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scenario planning and strategic options", "children": [{"id": "447ab074-f823-437c-a405-b5bb951a8df3", "type": "concept", "title": "Critical uncertainties, scenario matrix, narratives, quantitative implications, competitor behavior, stakeholder response, and signposts"}, {"id": "bb009b4f-4d9d-4d1b-92c3-3cb723b945c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Robust actions, contingent actions, no-regret moves, hedges, real options, staged commitments, and trigger points"}, {"id": "43e4f577-e632-43e2-84b1-6f4079614db0", "type": "concept", "title": "Wild cards, black swans, premortem, red team, sensitivity, decision trees, and continuous monitoring"}], "description": "This subtopic develops strategies that remain coherent across plausible futures and preserves choices when uncertainty is high."}, {"id": "9f2bb32e-85dd-4d05-a29a-fda768a7f246", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Resilience and crisis leadership", "children": [{"id": "a3f36c7b-74b9-4645-83a1-a5717b296d17", "type": "concept", "title": "Business impact analysis, critical services, dependencies, redundancy, backup, alternate suppliers, remote capability, and recovery objectives"}, {"id": "8122f640-e05e-4adb-beec-f7c6f2bd8447", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency command, safety, communication, guest care, employee support, cyber response, insurance, documentation, and stakeholder coordination"}, {"id": "c343c6e1-7d68-45ef-ba43-89fa4a74028d", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate adaptation, physical and transition risk, destination resilience, recovery sequencing, after-action learning, and trust repair"}], "description": "Students plan for continuity, adaptation, recovery, and renewal across properties, systems, people, suppliers, and destinations."}]}, {"id": "7cf4e1bc-e0de-404d-a34e-744f64ba092d", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethics, Corporate Governance, Stakeholders, and Sustainable Value", "description": "Hospitality strategy affects guests, employees, owners, communities, suppliers, destinations, and the environment. Students evaluate legitimacy, governance, distributional effects, and long-term value alongside financial performance.", "children": [{"id": "93b4145d-fe4f-41df-b911-f1790e3fb44a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethical strategic decision making", "children": [{"id": "ccbdb78b-4450-4665-8064-097df7ee63f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal minimum versus ethical responsibility, fiduciary duties, professional judgment, conflicts of interest, and speaking up"}, {"id": "7e72e317-d9c6-4c43-9652-28a4b9634a22", "type": "concept", "title": "Guest safety, labor practices, accessibility, discrimination, pricing fairness, privacy, surveillance, corruption, and human rights"}, {"id": "8ce8339e-27a4-410c-abe2-955484c22234", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholder mapping, options, harms/benefits, reversibility, precedent, transparency, challenge, escalation, and documentation"}], "description": "Students make values, duties, consequences, rights, justice, conflicts, and affected stakeholders explicit."}, {"id": "d08130d1-1f3d-4283-b23e-1455d0a92565", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Boards, owners, controls, and governance", "children": [{"id": "05db5053-83a8-4751-ae17-c467d421065a", "type": "concept", "title": "Board roles, independence, committees, executive authority, owner representation, franchise councils, and stakeholder forums"}, {"id": "8a853fac-5bd4-4aec-a4ea-5c9a96788026", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategy oversight, risk, succession, compensation, audit/internal control, cyber, data, AI, sustainability, and crisis governance"}, {"id": "9bae8974-5eb5-4e1f-9f6b-54d0d18278f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Information asymmetry, agency problems, related parties, incentives, misconduct, whistleblowing, assurance, and board evaluation"}], "description": "This subtopic clarifies oversight, accountability, incentives, information quality, and checks on managerial power."}, {"id": "6fe5eb08-18c6-4f88-a743-d1db0a271495", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sustainability, impact, and shared value", "children": [{"id": "96ca8fdf-4760-42a4-90cc-e295b624133f", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy, water, waste, food loss, carbon, biodiversity, sourcing, labor, community, housing, overtourism, and cultural heritage"}, {"id": "12a07583-088e-4368-92b8-d47f6ab39351", "type": "concept", "title": "Materiality, boundaries, baselines, targets, transition plans, life-cycle cost, circularity, resilience, and credible claims"}, {"id": "45f4be70-abff-434e-8c7c-6db082d4a07a", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholder outcomes, tradeoffs, distributional impact, greenwashing, measurement, assurance, policy, and value creation"}], "description": "Students integrate environmental and social dependencies into business-model, investment, supply-chain, and destination choices."}]}, {"id": "e30ca45a-df7b-4102-803c-ed4e01ce4f3d", "type": "topic", "title": "Integrative Hospitality Strategy Case and Board-Level Communication", "description": "The capstone requires students to diagnose a real or realistic enterprise, choose among credible alternatives, quantify consequences, design execution, and defend the recommendation under challenge.", "children": [{"id": "943d1f98-a833-469f-8bd5-b695f8097253", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case analysis and evidence synthesis", "children": [{"id": "a7a19cf0-90a3-4e99-9d4e-250c3e16a9a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Case facts, timeline, stakeholders, financial/operating data, market evidence, contracts, organization, and uncertainties"}, {"id": "dc3ee1d1-caa2-4add-95bb-8790e28288c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Issue tree, external/internal synthesis, business model, advantage, value drivers, risks, ethical dimensions, and assumptions"}, {"id": "cefaee6d-f845-43e2-b6b2-cf3694602358", "type": "concept", "title": "Materiality, triangulation, contradictory evidence, missing information, sensitivity, and confidence"}], "description": "Students move from a large evidence set to the few material issues that determine the decision."}, {"id": "909930bb-5c27-47dc-bf9f-3b0cc4896b0d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Alternatives and recommendation", "children": [{"id": "e0473439-f9aa-4f23-997a-46b1e40f8efc", "type": "concept", "title": "Status quo, improve, focus, grow, partner, acquire, divest, transform, stage, and exit alternatives"}, {"id": "c76bfd7b-8e33-4251-8891-fdd4da33b7d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Strategic fit, advantage, value, feasibility, capital, risk, resilience, ethics, stakeholder impact, timing, and reversibility"}, {"id": "07d99724-0ebb-472c-9b75-a3dc40e75d04", "type": "concept", "title": "Recommendation, rationale, rejected alternatives, conditions, milestones, contingencies, triggers, and kill criteria"}], "description": "This subtopic requires genuine options, explicit criteria, comparable economics, stakeholder consequences, and a coherent choice."}, {"id": "dce74450-4485-401d-a99d-7082527dcdd6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Board memo, presentation, and defense", "children": [{"id": "fa47e2a8-155b-4ece-9924-b1a34dc5baf6", "type": "concept", "title": "Executive summary, decision request, key exhibits, financial bridge, scenario table, implementation roadmap, and risk register"}, {"id": "7e0774e8-66cc-4ec8-a817-e7164a2c9bf1", "type": "concept", "title": "Pyramid structure, assertion-evidence slides, source notes, accessible visuals, assumptions, limitations, and appendix"}, {"id": "0757024d-527c-4151-aea9-c71fc870fe5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral briefing, team roles, Q&A, challenge response, negotiation, revision, decision log, and reflection"}], "description": "Students communicate with clarity, brevity, evidence, and readiness for skeptical questions."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$hstmjson$::jsonb,
